<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/intermediate/art-beyond-the-west">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Art beyond the West is <strong>most of the world\'s art history</strong> — with its own
    canons, theories and masterpieces, and its own relationship to the Western museum that
    collected much of it. The comparative frame (the anthropology lesson\'s method) replaces
    "influence on Europe" as the organising question.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Islamic, East Asian, South/Southeast Asian, Africa–Americas–Oceania" widget="equationStep" config={beyondCfg} />

  <h2>Reading across traditions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Function frames the reading:</strong> a Benin plaque records court history, a Native American mask participates in ceremony — Western categories ("art" vs "artefact") are themselves the artifact; the anthropology lesson\'s material-culture reading is the right lens.</li>
    <li><strong>The looting question is current:</strong> Benin bronzes (1897 punitive expedition), the Parthenon marbles, countless others — repatriation debates run case by case (the market lesson\'s provenance machinery, ethical edition); major museums have begun returning — the discipline\'s live reckoning.</li>
    <li><strong>Living traditions:</strong> calligraphy masters today, Haida carvers revitalising form-line, Aboriginal painting\'s contemporary movement (Papunya Tula — acrylic and Dreaming) — "non-Western" is a geography, never a tense: these are contemporary arts with deep pasts.</li>
    <li><strong>The comparative payoff:</strong> Chinese handscrolls unfold in TIME (the film lesson\'s grammar — a viewing experience, not an object), Islamic pattern privileges the infinite, African sculpture the conceptual essence — each tradition answers "what can images do?" differently, which is the historiography lesson\'s lesson.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rawson on non-Western art traditions; the anthropology lesson\'s archaeology and ethics; the British Museum/Benin restitution literature.</p>
